<h2>Part Two: The Language Lesson</h2>

<h3>1. What Is the Thai English Accent?</h3>
<p>The Thai English accent isn't "broken" English. It's English shaped by the rules of the Thai sound system — a phenomenon linguists call <strong>phonological transfer (L1 interference)</strong>.</p>
<p>Thai has 44 consonant letters and 32 vowel forms, but its inventory of actual sounds differs significantly from English. When Thai speakers encounter English sounds absent in their native language, their brains automatically substitute the closest Thai equivalent. Spanish, Mandarin, and French speakers do the exact same thing. The Thai English accent is simply one predictable, rule-governed variation of global English.</p>

