  <li><strong>Allies over solo advocacy:</strong> Collective documentation is harder to dismiss. Find others experiencing the same inversion.</li>
  <li><strong>Trust your body first:</strong> If something feels wrong, it is. Systems can be wrong; your nervous system cannot be gaslit into accuracy.</li>
  <li><strong>Set perceptual boundaries:</strong> Limit exposure to inverted systems when possible. Protect your cognitive bandwidth for spaces that honor input-output integrity.</li>
</ul>

<h3>For System Designers & Maintainers</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Acknowledge before diagnosing:</strong> "We believe you experienced inversion; we're investigating" validates before problem-solving.</li>
  <li><strong>Transparent root cause:</strong> Share findings openly. "Wiring reversed during last service" beats silence or defensiveness.</li>
  <li><strong>Prevent recurrence visibly:</strong> Post notice explaining fix + prevention steps. Demonstrates learning, not just repair.</li>
  <li><strong>Invite user testing:</strong> Ask affected users to verify fix. Restores agency and trust simultaneously.</li>
  <li><strong>Audit for hidden inversions:</strong> Review other systems for similar patterns. One inversion often indicates broader design neglect.</li>
</ul>

<!-- SECTION 5: BROADER IMPLICATIONS -->
<h2>5. Beyond the Lift: Inversion as Social Control Architecture</h2>
<p>Signal inversion isn't limited to machinery. It's embedded in social systems that benefit from confusion:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Bureaucracy:</strong> Forms that reject correct answers; approval processes that reverse decisions without explanation.</li>
  <li><strong>Relationships:</strong> Partners who punish honesty and reward compliance; apologies that reassign blame.</li>
  <li><strong>Workplaces:</strong> Feedback that contradicts performance metrics; promotions given to those who violate stated values.</li>
  <li><strong>Governance:</strong> Laws applied inversely to marginalized groups; rights granted conditionally based on obedience.</li>
</ul>
<p>Learning to recognize <strong>inversion as design</strong> rather than accident builds critical literacy applicable across all domains. The medium changes; the coercive function persists.</p>

<!-- CONCLUSION -->
<h2>Conclusion: When Down Means Up, Trust Yourself First</h2>
<p>An inverted lift can be faulty wiring. It can also be ambient gaslighting. The difference lives in acknowledgment, transparency, and respect for user perception.</p>
<p>Systems that honor input-output integrity build trust. Systems that invert signals erode it — deliberately or negligently. Naming the difference doesn't just get lifts fixed; it restores faith in your own capacity to perceive reality accurately.</p>

