<p><strong>"Under construction" isn't a status — it's a power dynamic.</strong></p>
<p>We've all encountered it: the website that's been "coming soon" for three years, the relationship perpetually "working on things," the workplace reform always "in progress." These aren't neutral placeholders. They're <strong>liminal traps</strong> — spaces where completion is promised but never delivered, keeping people invested in futures that may never materialize.</p>
<p>This post examines "under construction" states through the lens of <strong>suspended agency and coercive hope</strong>. Whether you're waiting on a partner who never follows through, stuck in organizational purgatory, or trying to understand why unfinished spaces feel so personally destabilizing, this guide gives you the framework to distinguish legitimate process from manipulative stalling — and reclaim your time, energy, and trust.</p>

<!-- SECTION 1: THE PSYCHOLOGY OF PERPETUAL INCOMPLETENESS -->
<h2>1. Why "Under Construction" Feels Like Betrayal</h2>

<h3>The Liminal Space Trap</h3>
<p>Liminality (from Latin <em>limen</em>, threshold) describes the disorienting space between what was and what will be. Anthropologist Victor Turner identified liminal spaces as sites of potential transformation — but also of <strong>structural vulnerability</strong>.</p>
<p>When "under construction" becomes permanent, three psychological harms emerge:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Suspended agency:</strong> You cannot act decisively because the rules, boundaries, or outcomes remain undefined. Decision-making paralysis replaces autonomy.</li>
  <li><strong>Investment without return:</strong> Time, emotion, and resources flow into a future that never arrives. Sunk cost fallacy deepens entrapment.</li>
  <li><strong>Erosion of trust calibration:</strong> Repeated broken promises degrade your ability to distinguish genuine effort from performative stalling. You begin doubting your own judgment about others' sincerity.</li>
</ul>

